---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ROLES } from '../lib/taxonomy';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { REPO_URL } from '../site.config';

const template = `---
title: "Example title"
description: "Short description"
roles:
  - software-engineer
tags:
  - cpp
  - cuda
  - gpu
  - profiling
author: "@exampleuser"
organisation: "Example University"
date: 2026-08-26
timeSensitive: false
sources:
  - title: "NVIDIA Nsight Compute documentation"
    url: "https://docs.nvidia.com/nsight-compute/"
---

## What we were trying to solve

One or two paragraphs of context...`;
---

<Base
  title="Contribute"
  description="How to submit a sustainability case study directly in your browser — no Git or command line needed."
>
  <div class="wrap section">
    <div class="section__head section__head--wide">
      <div class="stack" style="--stack-gap:.75rem">
        <h1>Contributing</h1>
        <p>Share your experiences of working on sustainability in your role.</p>
        <p>
          This site is only useful if it reflects what people are really doing — including the
          things that did not work! You only need a free GitHub account; everything can be done
          in your browser.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row" style="padding-top:.25rem">
          <a class="button button--primary" href={url('/contribute/write')}>
            Write a case study in your browser →
          </a>
          <a
            class="button button--ghost"
            href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/new?template=propose-article.yml`}
            rel="noopener"
          >
            Check an idea first
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="method-grid" style="max-width:var(--measure-wide)">
      <div class="method-card method-card--featured">
        <span class="eyebrow">Easiest · Visual editor</span>
        <h3>1. Use the browser editor</h3>
        <p>
          Fill in a guided form with live preview and automatic validation, then submit either as
          a <strong>draft GitHub Issue</strong> (to iterate with co-authors before publishing) or
          a <strong>direct pull request</strong>.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="button button--primary" href={url('/contribute/write')}>
            Open article editor
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="method-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">No forks or branches</span>
        <h3>2. Draft directly in a GitHub Issue</h3>
        <p>
          Prefer staying on GitHub without touching Git? Fill in the{' '}
          <strong>Start or submit an article</strong> issue form, edit the issue with co-authors
          as you go, and comment <code>/publish</code> when ready for a bot to open the review
          pull request.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a
            class="button button--ghost"
            href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/new?template=submit-article.yml`}
            rel="noopener"
          >
            Open draft issue form →
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose--wide">
      <h2>What makes a good article</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Be specific. Often sustainability advice is less practical because it is too generic.
        </li>
        <li>Say what it cost — effort, money, disruption, etc.</li>
        <li>Include anything that did not work.</li>
        <li>Cite any sources, especially for emissions, energy or cost figures.</li>
        <li>
          Only submit content you wrote or have the right to publish. Everything here is released
          under CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Roles</h2>
      <p>Who is this for? Pick 1–4 roles that would be most interested in your contribution.</p>
      <ul>
        {
          Object.entries(ROLES).map(([id, role]) => (
            <li>
              <code>{id}</code> ({role.label}): {role.description}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <h2>Commenting</h2>
      <p>
        Every article has a discussion thread using GitHub Discussions. Sign in with a GitHub
        account to add a question or comment.
      </p>

      <h2>Manual Git workflow &amp; file format reference</h2>
      <p>
        If you use the <a href={url('/contribute/write')}>browser editor</a> or the{' '}
        <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/new?template=submit-article.yml`} rel="noopener">
          draft issue form
        </a>, the YAML header and filename are generated for you automatically. If you prefer to
        work locally with Git and a text editor:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Fork the repository and create a branch.</li>
        <li>
          Copy <code>templates/article-template.md</code> to{' '}
          <code>content/case-studies/your-article-name.md</code> (lowercase words separated by
          hyphens).
        </li>
        <li>Fill in the YAML header and write the body in Markdown starting headings at <code>##</code>.</li>
        <li>Open a pull request and answer the checklist questions in the description.</li>
      </ol>

      <pre><code>{template}</code></pre>

      <h3>Frontmatter fields</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>title</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>8–100 characters.</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>description</code></td><td>Yes</td>
            <td>40–300 characters. Shown on cards and in search results.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>roles</code></td><td>Yes</td>
            <td>1–4 from the fixed list above.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>tags</code></td><td>Yes</td>
            <td>
              1–8, lowercase kebab-case. Reuse <a href={url('/tags')}>existing tags</a> where you can.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>author</code></td><td>Yes</td>
            <td>Your GitHub handle, or a list of handles for co-authors.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr><td><code>organisation</code></td><td>No</td><td>Institution or facility.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>date</code></td><td>Yes</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>updated</code></td><td>No</td>
            <td>Set on substantive revisions. Must not precede <code>date</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>timeSensitive</code></td><td>No</td>
            <td>
              <code>true</code> if the article cites specific tool versions, prices, etc. Renders
              a dated warning banner.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>sources</code></td><td>No</td>
            <td>Title/URL pairs. Strongly encouraged, especially for any figures you quote.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>draft</code></td><td>No</td>
            <td>
              <code>true</code> keeps it off the home page, browse, role and tag pages and the feed
              while you work. The article still gets its own unlisted web address you can send to a
              co-author for comments. Change to <code>false</code> to publish.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>draftId</code></td><td>Only for drafts</td>
            <td>
              Random characters added to a draft's web address. Run <code>npm run draft:id</code>,
              or type 6–16 random lowercase letters and digits. Unlisted is not private — anyone
              given the link or reading this repository can see the draft, so keep confidential
              material out of it.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
